          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 09:47:43 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop",
              "endpoint": "/api/Users/",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-916",
              "impact": "An attacker who gains read access to the user database (via SQL injection, backup exposure, or insider access) can reverse the majority of user password hashes in seconds using publicly available rainbow tables or GPU-accelerated cracking tools. MD5 hashes can be brute-forced at rates exceeding 50 billion hashes per second on modern hardware. Since no salt is applied, all users sharing the same password will have identical hashes, enabling instant bulk credential recovery.\n\nThis leads to:\n- Mass account compromise across the application\n- Credential reuse attacks against users' accounts on other platforms\n- Complete loss of password confidentiality even with database-only read access\n- Regulatory non-compliance (NIST SP 800-63B, OWASP ASVS, PCI DSS)",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerable hash function at `lib/insecurity.ts` line 43:\n\n```typescript\nexport const hash = (data: string) => crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex')\n```\n\nThis function is invoked in multiple critical contexts:\n- `models/user.ts` password setter (line ~53): stores hashed password on user creation and update\n- `routes/login.ts` (line ~35): hashes the submitted password for comparison during login\n- `routes/changePassword.ts` (line 39): hashes the current password for verification\n\nThe function produces a 128-bit (32 hex character) output with no salt, no key stretching, and no iteration count. MD5 has been considered cryptographically broken since 2004.\n\nRoot cause: Use of an obsolete, non-password-specific hash function without any of the countermeasures (salt, iteration, memory-hard computation) that modern password hashing algorithms provide.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace MD5 with a modern password-hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2id. These algorithms incorporate per-hash salts, configurable work factors, and memory-hard operations that resist GPU/ASIC acceleration.\n\n2. Implement the migration as follows:\n   - Add bcrypt (or argon2) as a dependency\n   - Create a new hash function using bcrypt with a minimum cost factor of 12\n   - On user login with a valid MD5-stored password, transparently rehash the password with bcrypt and update the database record\n   - Set a deadline after which MD5-only hashes are invalidated and users must reset passwords\n\n3. Ensure all password comparison uses constant-time comparison to prevent timing side-channels.\n\n4. Audit all other uses of the MD5 `hash()` function to confirm none are used for security-sensitive purposes beyond password storage.\n\n5. Update password policies to enforce minimum complexity requirements alongside the stronger hashing.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Examine the source code at `lib/insecurity.ts` line 43 to confirm MD5 is used.\n2. Register a user with a known password (e.g., \"admin123\").\n3. Query the Users table (via the SQL injection in the search endpoint or direct database access) to retrieve the stored hash.\n4. Confirm the hash matches the MD5 digest of the plaintext: `echo -n \"admin123\" | md5sum` yields `0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500`.\n5. Look up this hash on any public MD5 rainbow table service to instantly recover the original password.",
                "script": "import hashlib\n\n# Demonstrate that the application's hash function is plain MD5\ndef juice_shop_hash(data: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Replicates the hash function from lib/insecurity.ts\"\"\"\n    return hashlib.md5(data.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n# Known passwords from the application's default data\ntest_passwords = [\n    (\"admin123\", \"0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500\"),\n    (\"password\", \"5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99\"),\n    (\"test\", \"098f6bcd4621d373cade4e832627b4f6\"),\n]\n\nprint(\"Demonstrating MD5 password hash weakness:\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\n\nfor plaintext, expected_hash in test_passwords:\n    computed = juice_shop_hash(plaintext)\n    match = \"MATCH\" if computed == expected_hash else \"MISMATCH\"\n    print(f\"Password: '{plaintext}'\")\n    print(f\"  MD5 hash: {computed}\")\n    print(f\"  Expected: {expected_hash} [{match}]\")\n    print()\n\n# Demonstrate no salt - same password always produces same hash\nprint(\"Salt absence demonstration:\")\nprint(f\"  hash('admin123') = {juice_shop_hash('admin123')}\")\nprint(f\"  hash('admin123') = {juice_shop_hash('admin123')}\")\nprint(\"  Both identical - no per-user salt applied\")\nprint()\n\n# Demonstrate cracking speed context\nprint(\"Attack context:\")\nprint(\"  MD5 cracking rate: ~50 billion hashes/sec (RTX 4090)\")\nprint(\"  8-char alphanumeric keyspace: 2.8 trillion combinations\")\nprint(\"  Time to exhaust: ~56 seconds\")\nprint(\"  Rainbow table lookup: instant for common passwords\")\nprint()\nprint(\"CONFIRMED: Passwords stored with unsalted MD5 - trivially reversible\")"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
